<div id="A2.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Motion Trajectory.</span> As mentioned in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Motion Reconstruction ‣ 3 Method ‣ See&amp;Trek: Training-Free Spatial Prompting for Multimodal Large Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, we also investigate performance impact of different part of input visual trajectories containing BEV <math id="A2.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}_{\text{BEV}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mtext>BEV</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}_{\text{BEV}}</annotation></semantics></math> and 3D figures <math id="A2.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}_{\text{3D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mtext>3D</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}_{\text{3D}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Table <a href="#A2.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ B.4 Ablation Study ‣ Appendix B Experiments ‣ See&amp;Trek: Training-Free Spatial Prompting for Multimodal Large Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> presents the ablation results evaluating the contribution of different visual trajectory components—BEV projections (<math id="A2.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}_{\text{BEV}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mtext>BEV</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}_{\text{BEV}}</annotation></semantics></math>) and 3D spatial figures (<math id="A2.SS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}_{\text{3D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mtext>3D</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}_{\text{3D}}</annotation></semantics></math>) to the overall performance. Removing either component leads to a noticeable drop in average accuracy, confirming the complementary value of both trajectory views in enhancing spatial understanding. Specifically, excluding <math id="A2.SS4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}_{\text{BEV}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mtext>BEV</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}_{\text{BEV}}</annotation></semantics></math> results in slightly better performance than removing <math id="A2.SS4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}_{\text{3D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mtext>3D</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}_{\text{3D}}</annotation></semantics></math>, particularly in tasks requiring high-level route planning and temporal ordering, suggesting that BEV provides a more intuitive overview of the navigation path and spatial layout. Conversely, the 3D visualization offers critical depth and geometric cues, as evidenced by its impact on fine-grained spatial tasks such as "Obj. Size" and "Room Size." The results demonstrate that both <math id="A2.SS4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}_{\text{BEV}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mtext>BEV</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}_{\text{BEV}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}_{\text{3D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mtext>3D</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}_{\text{3D}}</annotation></semantics></math> are indispensable for supplying diverse and complementary motion context to the <span id="A2.SS4.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">MLLM</span>, enabling more robust reasoning across spatial tasks.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T11" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 11: </span>Comparison results of different visual spatial methods containing <span id="A2.T11.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SoM<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite></span>, <span id="A2.T11.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CC</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite> and our <span id="A2.T11.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">See&amp;Trek</span>. It shows that <span id="A2.T11.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">See&amp;Trek</span> obtains the SOTA performance in boosting the MLLMs spatial understanding.</figcaption>
